WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
Book 1 · Athena and Telemachus

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:15.220
<v Narrator><i>The gods take their places in a widening ring. Poseidon’s seat remains empty.</i>

3
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:43.110
<v Athena>Father Zeus, let Aegisthus lie in the ruin he earned. It is Odysseus my heart breaks for, wise, unlucky Odysseus, held against his will on Calypso's island, longing only for the sight of smoke rising from his own hearth. Has he not paid you sacrifice enough on the plain of Troy, Olympian?

4
00:00:43.160 --> 00:01:13.520
<v Zeus>My child, how could I forget godlike Odysseus? It is Poseidon the earth-shaker who rages without rest for the blinding of his Cyclops son, and drives that man from his homecoming. But come, let us here shape his return; one god cannot hold out against us all.

5
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:31.817
<v Athena>Then send Hermes, the guide, the slayer of Argus, across the water to Ogygia at once. Let him stand before the nymph Calypso with our fixed decree: hard-enduring Odysseus goes home.

6
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:52.587
<v Athena>And I go to Ithaca myself, to breathe strength into his son. I will set Telemachus on his feet, send him to call the Achaeans to assembly, and then across the sea to Pylos and Sparta for news of his father, so the boy may win a name among men.

7
00:01:52.637 --> 00:02:03.517
<v Narrator><i>Athena fastens the gold sandals, closes her hand around the spear, and drops from Olympus.</i>

8
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:15.097
<v Narrator><i>Wearing the shape of Mentes, Athena waits at the palace threshold with her spear upright.</i>

9
00:02:15.147 --> 00:02:24.747
<v Narrator><i>Dice strike the floor; cups are filled; the suitors never look toward the gate.</i>

10
00:02:24.797 --> 00:02:35.517
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus sees the unattended stranger and crosses the hall, ashamed of his own house.</i>

11
00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:47.727
<v Telemachus>Welcome, stranger. You are kindly come to our house; give me your spear. Eat first, and afterward tell me what you need, but come, away from this noise.

12
00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:50.167
Book 2 · The Assembly and the Voyage

13
00:02:50.517 --> 00:03:11.437
<v Antinous>Bold-tongued Telemachus, do not lay your blame at our door. It is not the suitors who are at fault but your own mother, that queen of cunning; three years now, and nearly four, she has cheated the hearts of the Achaeans.

14
00:03:11.487 --> 00:03:40.247
<v Antinous>She set up her great loom in the hall, a shroud, she told us, for the lord Laertes, so no woman of the land could reproach her if he lay in death without a winding-sheet. All day she wove at that web, and every night, with torches set beside her, she unraveled it thread by thread.

15
00:03:40.297 --> 00:03:49.697
<v Antinous>Send her back to her father Icarius, and let her marry the man he names, and the man she chooses.

16
00:03:49.417 --> 00:03:52.167
Book 3 · Nestor at Pylos

17
00:03:52.517 --> 00:04:17.327
<v Athena>Go with him, Telemachus, and sleep at the king's house. But I will back to the ship, to hearten the crew, for I am the one old man among them; and at dawn I go on to the Cauconians, where a debt is owed me, no new thing and no small one.

18
00:04:17.377 --> 00:04:56.987
<v Nestor>Dear boy, no fear that you prove base or weak, when gods walk beside you so young, and that was no other of the Olympians but the daughter of Zeus himself, the glorious grey-eyed one who honored your father among the Argives. Be gracious, mistress: grant fair fame to me, to my children, to my honored wife, and I will sacrifice to you a yearling heifer, broad-browed, unbroken, her horns wrapped in gold.

19
00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:59.417
Book 4 · Menelaus and Helen at Sparta

20
00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:18.487
<v Menelaus>True, wife — yet you tell only half. Hear the horse's tale from within: I crouched in that timber belly with the best of the Argives, and Odysseus held command of us all, when you came circling in the dark.

21
00:05:18.537 --> 00:05:27.297
<v Narrator><i>Helen circled the horse and imitated the voices of the warriors' wives.</i>

22
00:05:27.347 --> 00:05:32.747
<v Narrator><i>The hidden Greeks nearly answered.</i>

23
00:05:32.797 --> 00:05:38.797
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus restrained them and silenced Anticlus.</i>

24
00:05:38.847 --> 00:05:46.687
<v Narrator><i>Telemachus hears proof of his father's endurance and command under pressure.</i>

25
00:05:47.183 --> 00:05:57.103
<v Narrator><i>Menelaus and three men hid beneath sealskins and grappled the Old Man of the Sea.</i>

26
00:05:57.153 --> 00:06:06.913
<v Narrator><i>Proteus changed into beasts, water, and a tree before yielding prophecy.</i>

27
00:06:06.963 --> 00:06:16.523
<v Narrator><i>Proteus revealed that Odysseus lived, detained by Calypso on an island.</i>

28
00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:19.000
Book 5 · Calypso and the Raft

29
00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:47.150
<v Calypso>Here at the island's edge the tall trees stand — alder and poplar and sky-high fir, long dry, that will ride light on the water. Take the great bronze axe and the adze; here is cloth for a sail and cord to lace her. Build, and I will bring all the rest.

30
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:59.360
<v Narrator><i>Calypso fells, trims, bores, joins, decks, and fences the timbers over four days.</i>

31
00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:08.610
<v Narrator><i>Calypso rigs mast, yard, sail, steering oar, and ballast.</i>

32
00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:20.460
<v Narrator><i>Calypso bathes and clothes him, loads food and water, and sends a warm wind behind the raft.</i>

33
00:07:20.933 --> 00:07:31.933
<v Narrator><i>For seventeen days Odysseus steers by the stars until Scheria rises ahead.</i>

34
00:07:31.983 --> 00:07:41.943
<v Narrator><i>Poseidon sees him, gathers clouds, and strikes the sea with a four-wind storm.</i>

35
00:07:41.993 --> 00:07:46.713
<v Narrator><i>The raft rolls.</i>

36
00:07:46.763 --> 00:07:53.123
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus is swept away and barely regains it.</i>

37
00:07:53.173 --> 00:07:57.613
<v Narrator><i>Abandon the raft and swim.</i>

38
00:07:57.663 --> 00:08:07.383
<v Narrator><i>Athena restrains the winds except the north wind that drives him shoreward.</i>

39
00:08:07.083 --> 00:08:09.833
Book 6 · Nausicaa

40
00:08:10.183 --> 00:08:19.463
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus debates whether to clasp Nausicaa's knees or speak from a distance.</i>

41
00:08:19.513 --> 00:08:52.473
<v Nausicaa>Stranger, you seem neither base nor witless, and Zeus deals fortune as he wills — what he gives, a man must bear. But you have come to our land now, and you shall lack neither clothing nor anything owed a hard-tried suppliant. Stand, girls — this man is no enemy; wash him where the river gives shelter, and set food and oil beside him.

42
00:08:52.167 --> 00:08:54.917
Book 7 · The Palace of Alcinous

43
00:08:55.267 --> 00:09:03.107
<v Narrator><i>Athena's mist falls away only when Odysseus reaches Arete.</i>

44
00:09:03.157 --> 00:09:19.514
<v Odysseus>Arete, daughter of Rhexenor, after many trials I come to your knees, and to your king, and to all these feasters. May the gods give you long joy. For me, ask only this: grant me convoy to my own country, for I have suffered long, far from everyone I love.

45
00:09:19.564 --> 00:09:43.564
<v Echeneus>Alcinous, this is not well, and it does not befit you: a stranger sitting in the ashes of your hearth. Raise him up, seat him on a silver-studded chair, and let the herald mix wine, for Zeus himself walks with suppliants.

46
00:09:43.614 --> 00:09:52.694
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus is seated beside the king, fed, and promised a convoy home.</i>

47
00:09:52.417 --> 00:09:55.167
Book 8 · Songs and Games among the Phaeacians

48
00:09:55.517 --> 00:10:05.797
<v Narrator><i>Sing now: the Greeks' emergence, the sack, and Odysseus's attack on Deiphobus's house.</i>

49
00:10:05.847 --> 00:10:13.727
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus weeps like a captive woman over a fallen husband.</i>

50
00:10:13.777 --> 00:10:44.617
<v Alcinous>Stop the lyre, Demodocus — not all here take joy of it, for since the song began our guest has not once left off weeping. Now, stranger, hide it no longer: tell me the name your father and mother called you, and your land and your city. And say why you weep at the doom of the Argives and Troy — did some kinsman fall there, or a comrade dear as a brother?

51
00:10:44.667 --> 00:10:51.187
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus prepares to narrate his wanderings.</i>

52
00:10:50.917 --> 00:10:53.667
Book 9 · The Cyclops

53
00:10:54.017 --> 00:11:01.337
<v Narrator><i>A north wind drives the ships off course for nine days.</i>

54
00:11:01.387 --> 00:11:09.547
<v Narrator><i>They land among the Lotus-eaters and send three scouts inland.</i>

55
00:11:09.597 --> 00:11:14.437
<v Narrator><i>The inhabitants offer lotus.</i>

56
00:11:14.487 --> 00:11:21.567
<v Narrator><i>Those who taste it lose all desire for home.</i>

57
00:11:21.617 --> 00:11:36.337
<v Odysseus>Take them, though they weep — bind them under the benches! The rest of you aboard and to your oars now, before another man tastes the lotus and forgets his home.

58
00:11:36.767 --> 00:11:47.927
<v Narrator><i>At dusk Polyphemus returns carrying a massive load of wood and drives his flock inside.</i>

59
00:11:47.977 --> 00:11:59.417
<v Narrator><i>Polyphemus lifts a stone slab that no ordinary team could move and blocks the entrance.</i>

60
00:11:59.467 --> 00:12:11.387
<v Narrator><i>Polyphemus milks ewes and goats, sets young beneath mothers, curdles milk, and lights a fire.</i>

61
00:12:11.437 --> 00:12:18.197
<v Narrator><i>The hidden men and the giant demands their identity.</i>

62
00:12:18.683 --> 00:12:29.923
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus considers killing the sleeping giant but realizes the entrance stone would trap them forever.</i>

63
00:12:29.973 --> 00:12:41.133
<v Narrator><i>At dawn Polyphemus eats two more men and leaves with the flock, sealing the cave again.</i>

64
00:12:41.183 --> 00:12:53.703
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus selects a green olive trunk, cuts and smooths it into a stake, and hardens its point in the fire.</i>

65
00:12:53.753 --> 00:13:02.033
<v Narrator><i>The men hide the stake beneath dung and draw lots for four helpers.</i>

66
00:13:02.083 --> 00:13:25.923
<v Odysseus>Here, Cyclops — drink wine on that man-flesh you have eaten, and learn what drink our ship carried. You ask my name, and I will tell it for the guest-gift you promised: Nobody is my name. Nobody — so my father and mother and all my friends call me.

67
00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:55.510
<v Polyphemus>Give me more, freely — and here is your gift, Nobody, to warm your heart: I eat Nobody last, after all his friends. There is my guest-gift to you.

68
00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:07.080
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus heats the stake until it glows and the chosen men drive it into the Cyclops's eye.</i>

69
00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:14.290
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus twist it as blood and steam erupt.</i>

70
00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:20.740
<v Narrator><i>Polyphemus wakes with a roar and tears it free.</i>

71
00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:46.230
<v Cyclopes outside>What ails you, Polyphemus, that you wake the deathless night with screaming — is some man stealing your flocks, or killing you by craft or force? If Nobody harms you, alone as you are, it is sickness sent by Zeus, and cannot be helped — pray to your father Poseidon.

72
00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:54.960
<v Narrator><i>Polyphemus gropes at the entrance while the men hide among the animals.</i>

73
00:14:55.433 --> 00:15:05.713
<v Narrator><i>At dawn Polyphemus opens the cave and feels every animal's back as it passes.</i>

74
00:15:05.763 --> 00:15:18.203
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus binds three rams abreast for each man, suspending a sailor beneath the middle animal.</i>

75
00:15:18.253 --> 00:15:48.733
<v Odysseus>I took the great lead ram myself and hung beneath his belly, my hands twisted in that miracle of wool, and dared not breathe. Over my head the blind master mourned to him: Sweet ram, why last from the cave today, you who were always first — do you grieve for your master's eye?

76
00:15:48.783 --> 00:15:58.663
<v Narrator><i>Outside, the men release themselves, drive the flock to the ship, and row away.</i>

77
00:15:58.713 --> 00:16:07.073
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus divide the animals and sacrifice the great ram to Zeus.</i>

78
00:16:07.517 --> 00:16:24.597
<v Odysseus>Cyclops! It was no weak man's friends you ate in your hollow cave — your evil deeds were bound to find you out! You dared devour guests in your own house, and Zeus and the gods have paid you for it!

79
00:16:24.647 --> 00:16:35.607
<v Narrator><i>Polyphemus tears off a mountain peak and hurls it, driving a wave that nearly beaches the ship again.</i>

80
00:16:35.657 --> 00:16:54.217
<v Odysseus>Cyclops — if any mortal ever asks who put out your eye so shamefully, tell him this: it was Odysseus, sacker of cities, who blinded you — Laertes's son, whose home is in Ithaca!

81
00:16:54.267 --> 00:17:39.347
<v Polyphemus>So the old prophecy comes home — Telemus foretold I would lose my sight at the hands of Odysseus, and I watched all these years for a great man, not this little worthless nothing who undid me with wine. Hear me, Poseidon, dark-haired shaker of the earth, if I am truly your son: grant that Odysseus, sacker of cities, never reach his home. Or if he must, let him come late and broken, on a stranger's ship, all his comrades lost — and find trouble waiting in his house.

82
00:17:39.397 --> 00:17:47.237
<v Narrator><i>A second rock misses as the ship escapes to the fleet.</i>

83
00:17:46.917 --> 00:17:49.667
Book 10 · Aeolus, the Laestrygonians and Circe

84
00:17:50.017 --> 00:18:06.097
<v Aeolus>Take this bag of oxhide, Odysseus — every rough wind is bound inside, sewn shut with silver wire. I loose the West Wind only; let it carry you home to Ithaca.

85
00:18:06.147 --> 00:18:20.747
<v Aeolus>Out of my island — out, worst of living men! You come back cursed; the blessed gods themselves hate you. I give no help to what heaven hunts.

86
00:18:21.183 --> 00:18:28.703
<v Narrator><i>The fleet enters a narrow harbor ringed by cliffs.</i>

87
00:18:28.753 --> 00:18:35.433
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus alone moors outside.</i>

88
00:18:35.483 --> 00:18:38.963
<v Narrator><i>Your parents' house.</i>

89
00:18:39.013 --> 00:18:47.813
<v Narrator><i>The queen summons Antiphates, who seizes and eats one scout.</i>

90
00:18:47.863 --> 00:18:55.343
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus cuts his cable and escapes with only his own ship.</i>

91
00:18:55.850 --> 00:19:06.890
<v Narrator><i>The scouts find Circe singing at her loom while enchanted wolves and lions fawn around them.</i>

92
00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:11.380
<v Narrator><i>All but Eurylochus enter.</i>

93
00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:19.270
<v Narrator><i>Circe serves drugged food and strikes them with her wand.</i>

94
00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:26.920
<v Narrator><i>The men become pigs in body while retaining human minds.</i>

95
00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:33.330
<v Narrator><i>Circe shuts them in pens and throws them acorns.</i>

96
00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:47.140
<v Eurylochus>They are gone, Odysseus — all of them! They went in at the shining doors and not one came out. I waited long, and called, and no man answered.

97
00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:57.800
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus arms himself and heads alone toward Circe's house despite Eurylochus's protest.</i>

98
00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:15.450
<v Hermes>Where now, unlucky man, alone through these hills and knowing nothing of the ground? Your friends are penned as pigs in Circe's sties — and you march straight in to join them.

99
00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:35.020
<v Hermes>Here — take this herb of power and carry it against her drugs. The gods call it moly: black at the root, its flower white as milk. Hard for mortal men to dig, but the gods can do all things.

100
00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:54.910
<v Hermes>When she strikes you with her wand, draw your sword and rush her as if to kill. And before you go to her bed, make her swear the great oath of the gods that she plots no further harm — lest she strip you naked and unman you.

101
00:20:54.583 --> 00:20:57.333
Book 11 · The Dead

102
00:20:57.683 --> 00:21:06.283
<v Narrator><i>The ship reaches the misty land of the Cimmerians at Ocean's edge.</i>

103
00:21:06.333 --> 00:21:20.653
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus digs a trench, pours libations, sacrifices sheep, and holds the spirits back with his sword.</i>

104
00:21:20.703 --> 00:21:29.343
<v Narrator><i>The dead gather in multitudes, crying around the blood.</i>

105
00:21:29.393 --> 00:21:38.433
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus waits for Tiresias before allowing any other shade to drink.</i>

106
00:21:38.933 --> 00:21:50.773
<v Odysseus>Come, mother — drink of the blood and know me. It is I, alive, your son.

107
00:21:50.823 --> 00:22:02.663
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus describes Penelope's fidelity, Telemachus's position, and Laertes's isolated grief.</i>

108
00:22:02.713 --> 00:22:20.553
<v Odysseus>No sickness took you, then. It was longing — longing for me, for my counsels and my gentleness — that stole the honey of your life. My going killed you, mother.

109
00:22:20.603 --> 00:22:31.603
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus tries three times to embrace her, but his arms pass through the shade.</i>

110
00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:40.340
<v Narrator><i>Achilles approaches with Patroclus, Antilochus, and Ajax nearby.</i>

111
00:22:40.390 --> 00:23:00.550
<v Odysseus>Achilles, no man was ever more blessed, nor will be: alive we honored you like a god, and here you rule with power among the dead. Do not grieve at dying — you of all men.

112
00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:12.733
<v Achilles>Say not a word in death's favour, Odysseus. I would rather be a hired man, alive, working the fields of some poor farmer with little to live on, than rule over all the dead who have perished.

113
00:23:12.783 --> 00:23:29.383
<v Odysseus>Would you hear of Peleus, and of your son Neoptolemus? Of your father I know nothing — but of the boy I can speak truly, for I myself brought him from Scyros to the war.

114
00:23:29.433 --> 00:23:35.113
<v Narrator><i>Achilles strides away rejoicing.</i>

115
00:23:34.667 --> 00:23:37.417
Book 12 · The Sirens, Scylla and the Cattle

116
00:23:37.767 --> 00:23:45.167
<v Narrator><i>The wind dies as the ship approaches the Sirens' island.</i>

117
00:23:45.217 --> 00:23:53.017
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus softens wax and seals every crewman's ears.</i>

118
00:23:53.067 --> 00:24:02.627
<v Narrator><i>The men bind him upright to the mast and row past the flowered shore.</i>

119
00:24:02.677 --> 00:24:08.637
<v Narrator><i>The Sirens promise total knowledge.</i>

120
00:24:08.687 --> 00:24:14.447
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus struggles and signals to be released.</i>

121
00:24:14.497 --> 00:24:24.297
<v Narrator><i>Perimedes and Eurylochus tighten the ropes until the song fades.</i>

122
00:24:24.767 --> 00:24:35.047
<v Narrator><i>The crew hears Charybdis roar and sees spray rising from the whirlpool.</i>

123
00:24:35.097 --> 00:24:49.857
<v Odysseus>Row, friends — we have been in worse and lived, and this too we shall remember. Helmsman, hold her off that smoke and hug the cliff — keep to the rock, or you kill us all.

124
00:24:49.907 --> 00:24:58.427
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus arms himself despite Circe's warning and searches the rock face.</i>

125
00:24:58.477 --> 00:25:06.717
<v Narrator><i>Scylla's six heads strike, lifting six screaming men from the ship.</i>

126
00:25:06.767 --> 00:25:17.487
<v Narrator><i>The survivors pass beyond both monsters while watching their companions die above them.</i>

127
00:25:17.933 --> 00:25:27.253
<v Narrator><i>Eurylochus persuades the crew to sacrifice the best cattle rather than starve.</i>

128
00:25:27.303 --> 00:25:40.023
<v Narrator><i>Eurylochus substitute oak leaves for barley and water for wine, kill the animals, and roast the meat.</i>

129
00:25:40.073 --> 00:25:47.313
<v Narrator><i>The hides crawl and the meat bellows on the spits.</i>

130
00:25:47.363 --> 00:25:55.323
<v Narrator><i>Lampetie tells Helios, who demands punishment from Zeus.</i>

131
00:25:55.767 --> 00:26:13.207
<v Odysseus>What have you done — you have slaughtered the Sun's own herds while I slept! O father Zeus, you lulled me to my ruin. This is doom, and no offering on earth will unmake it.

132
00:26:13.257 --> 00:26:22.617
<v Narrator><i>On the seventh day the wind falls and the ship leaves Thrinacia.</i>

133
00:26:22.667 --> 00:26:29.627
<v Narrator><i>Zeus gathers a black cloud and strikes the mast with lightning.</i>

134
00:26:29.677 --> 00:26:36.677
<v Narrator><i>The ship shatters and every crewman drowns.</i>

135
00:26:36.727 --> 00:26:42.487
<v Narrator><i>Odysseus lashes keel and mast together.</i>

136
00:26:42.537 --> 00:26:55.697
<v Narrator><i>Driven back to Charybdis, he hangs from the fig tree until the timbers reemerge, then drifts alone to Calypso.</i>
